Account of Meha Dhimar, Disciple of Shri Gusainji

Meha Dhimar, a fisherman, lived on the banks of Shri Yamunaji at Gopalpur near Raval. Once, Shri Gusainji traveled from Gujarat to Shri Gokul and camped at the ghat of Gopalpur, as that day was not an auspicious time to enter Gokul. Meha Dhimar was sitting by the bank of Yamunaji to catch fish. A Vaishnav said to him, 'I will give you one rupee; do not cast your net as long as Shri Gusainji is staying here.' Meha took the rupee, went home, and asked the Vaishnav to also arrange for some food for him. After Shri Gusainji had dined and Mahaprasad plates were served to all the Vaishnavs, that Vaishnav asked the Brajvasis to summon Meha Dhimar from the village.

When Meha Dhimar arrived and had darshan of Shri Gusainji, he beheld Him as the manifest Supreme Lord (Purna Purushottam) and prayed, 'Maharaj, please take me into Your shelter.' Shri Gusainji graciously initiated him and his entire household with the sacred Name. Instantly, Meha gained realization of Shri Gusainji's divine form and sang this pad:

Raag Bhairav
Shri Vitthal Prabhu is supremely munificent;
He accepted the most fallen into His shelter and surely granted the fearless abode. (1)
That Purushottam, called the essence of the Vedas and Puranas, placed His hand upon me;
Meha's Lord Giridhar manifested and revealed the nectar of Pushtimarg. (2)

Hearing this pad, Shri Gusainji was deeply pleased, and the Vaishnavs marveled, 'This morning Meha was sitting with his fishing net, and by a single darshan how pure his intellect has become! There is no limit to the Lord's grace.' Shri Gusainji then offered him His own sacred remnants (joothan), upon partaking of which Meha experienced the divine Leela. He accompanied Shri Gusainji to Shri Gokul, received a Swaroop of Shri Thakurji, and began performing Seva.

Later, during the Annakut festival, he came for Shrinathji's darshan and sang these Annakut pads:

Raag Sarang
Our deity is Mount Govardhan, where the cattle find joy;
Offer no share of the sacrifice to Indra; follow our counsel instead. (1)
Fire, wind, moon, water, and sun all obey the Mountain's command;
Do as that Supreme Lord bids—what is gained by offering to Indra? (2)
Around Him all the Braj families dwell happily under the Lord's protection;
Load the carts with untouched offerings—who would reject such good counsel? (3)
Let the elders sit and deliberate, and make the offering to the Mountain;
Do whatever Nandlal's beloved darling Kanha says. (4)
Butter, milk, curds, ghee, and kheer—all the Brajvasis bring them forth;
Assuming the form of the sacrifice itself, the Mountain-dweller partakes of the offerings. (5)
Realizing his share was lost, Indra unleashed the storm clouds;
Meha's joy-giving Lord Giridhar, son of Nanda, held the Mountain aloft on His hand. (6)

Raag Sarang
Listen, Father, to my counsel: perform the worship of Shri Govardhan;
Bring here and offer the sacrifice you prepared for the king of the gods. (1)
He is a treasure of roots, fruits, and flowers—whatever you ask, you receive;
This mountain is our home; graze the cows here fearlessly day and night. (2)
Vats of milk and curds were filled, along with boundless nectar-like delicacies;
Trays were heaped high with honey, dried fruits, savories, and sweets. (3)
Let the elders sit and agree: do just as Kanha says;
Prepare a varied Annakut and offer it to the Mountain. (4)
This mountain assumes manifold forms and protects the people of Braj;
Among the gods He is the greatest deity, and exceedingly dear to me. (5)
Nanda's son assumed another form Himself and partook of the feast;
Meha's darling Lord Giridhar asked for more and more and accepted it all. (6)

Upon finishing these pads, Meha fainted in ecstasy. Shri Gusainji cast a gracious glance upon him, raised him up, and said, 'It is Shrinathji's command that you remain on earth for some more days.' Hearing this, Meha rose, offered dandavat prostrations, said 'Nijecchatah karishyati' ('He shall do as He wills'), and devoted himself to Shri Thakurji's Seva.

Meha returned to Gopalpur to continue his Seva. Later, his wife became pregnant, and when the time of delivery came, Meha was away from the village. A son was born to her. Meha's wife felt great distress and began to weep, lamenting, 'Why was this troublesome son born? Because of this, my Bhagavat-seva has been interrupted!' Thereupon Shri Thakurji Himself spoke to her, 'Do not weep; bathe and perform My Seva.' Following the proper rite, she bathed and performed Seva. When Meha returned and asked, 'Why did you perform Seva in this state?' she replied, 'Shri Thakurji Himself commanded me.' Hearing this, Meha was overjoyed. Meha composed many new pads singing the divine Leela in manifold ways. Such a deeply favored Bhagavadiya of Shri Gusainji was Meha.
